# Summary
A new assert in PG hashing assumes validated input sizes; copy-source header parsing does not enforce those limits, so oversized copy-source values now panic the server and cause a remote DoS.
The commit replaces PG hashing with a stack-buffered `hash_parts` helper that asserts the combined bucket/key/version length fits `PG_HASH_STACK_LIMIT`. This is safe only if all callers validate bucket/key lengths. However, `parse_copy_source` percent-decodes `x-amz-copy-source` without applying `validate_bucket_name`/`validate_object_key`, allowing bucket or key strings longer than the 63/1024 byte limits. When CopyObject or UploadPartCopy uses these unvalidated values, the coordinator ultimately hashes them for PG selection; the new assert will panic, crashing the worker or process. An authenticated (or public-write) client can therefore trigger a denial-of-service by sending a long copy-source header within the header-size limit. The issue is introduced by this commit because the previous hashing path did not assert on length.

